<Project>

  <!--
    Inherited by every project under this directory, so the C# ruleset in
    .editorconfig applies to the whole solution from the first project onward
    (ADR-0065).

    This file exists because severity is not enough. Measured on 2026-08-02
    against .NET SDK 10.0.301: an .editorconfig carrying
    `dotnet_diagnostic.IDE1006.severity = error` and
    `dotnet_diagnostic.IDE0055.severity = error`, against a file violating both,
    produced `Build succeeded` and exit 0. Setting the property below turned the
    same build into `Build FAILED` and exit 1. ADR-0065's "set to error severity
    so a violation fails the build" is therefore a statement about the pair, and
    landing the .editorconfig without this file would have shipped a ruleset that
    reads as enforced and is not.

    Until 2026-08-02 this was deliberately one property, because the rest had no
    projects to configure. The first project landed that day and brought the
    target-framework knob with it (ADR-0030 parameter C). MinVer and the
    reproducible-build stack are still ADR-0030's and still absent, and the
    public-API analyzers are still ADR-0044's.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    RS0017 at error (ADR-0044 parameter A), landed 2026-08-02.

    Its two siblings, RS0016 and RS0037, are set in .editorconfig where a
    severity belongs. This one is here because .editorconfig cannot reach it,
    which was found by measurement rather than expected.

    A diagnostic's severity is matched against the file it is REPORTED IN, and
    RS0017's subject is a stale line in the API text file, so it is reported at
    `PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt(N,1)` and not in any `.cs`. Measured 2026-08-02 on
    SDK 10.0.301 with analyzer 5.6.0, four placements were tried and four left
    it at its default of warning: `[*.cs]`, a section naming the two API files
    explicitly, `[*]`, and a root `.globalconfig` added through an MSBuild item.
    A `.globalconfig` in the project's own directory did work, and is rejected
    because it is per-project and would have to be copied into every package.
    This property is one line, inherited, and measured to exit 1.

    Do not "tidy" this into .editorconfig for symmetry. The symmetry is the bug.

    Why RS0017 specifically has to bite: it is the only one of the three that
    fires on a pure removal, a public member deleted from the code with its
    lines left in the API file. Measured while it was still a warning, that
    produced `2 Warning(s)`, `Build succeeded`, exit 0, which is the
    MAJOR-breaking direction of ADR-0044 parameter B passing a gate that read
    as configured.

    The $(WarningsAsErrors) prefix preserves anything a project or a future
    property sets, so this appends rather than replaces. When this landed on
    2026-08-02, TreatWarningsAsErrors was not set and this was deliberately not
    the beginning of it: it promotes one named diagnostic that an accepted ADR
    already calls an error. TreatWarningsAsErrors arrived separately on
    2026-08-03 under ADR-0093, in the last group in this file, and it does not
    make this line redundant: RS0017 is promoted by name, so it stays an error
    even for a project that carves something out.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);RS0017</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The target-framework knob (ADR-0030 parameter C).

    TWO properties, not one, and the reason is parameter B rather than taste: a
    published library multi-targets the current and next LTS while the host is
    single-target on the current one. Both read net10.0 today because .NET 12 has
    not shipped, so the difference is invisible and the shape is the only thing
    that can be got right in advance. When 12 ships, the library property becomes
    `net10.0;net12.0` and the application property does not move.

    LangVersion is deliberately ABSENT even though parameter C names it, and this
    is the one deviation in this file. Measured on SDK 10.0.301 with no
    Directory.Build.props at all, a net10.0 project already reports
    LangVersion 14.0: the default is derived from the target framework, so the
    knob above already centralizes it. Writing it here could only restate that
    default, which rots against the SDK, or set `latest`, which would make the
    language version float with whatever SDK is installed and break the very
    coupling to the target framework that parameter C wants. This applies
    ADR-0065's "write only what deviates" rule, which that ADR states about
    .editorconfig, to a second file. Recorded in ADR-0030 rather than only here.

    Nullable is enabled because it is load-bearing rather than tidy: design 23
    section 3 annotates its definition model with `string?` on some members and
    not others, and that distinction means nothing to the compiler unless this is
    on. ADR-0044 parameter A also puts nullable annotations in the public API
    file, which needs the same. Measured default: off.

    ImplicitUsings is enabled on the design corpus's foundations task 1.4, which
    is the only source that states it. Measured default: off.

    TreatWarningsAsErrors was WITHHELD here on 2026-08-02 and is set in the
    group below from 2026-08-03. The reason for withholding it is kept because
    it is the record of what changed rather than of what was decided: the
    corpus task 1.4 asked for it, no ADR did, and turning it on in the same
    change that landed the first project would have made every future warning a
    build break before anyone had seen what warnings this codebase produces.
    What resolved that was seeing them. Measured 2026-08-03 on SDK 10.0.301,
    the solution builds `0 Warning(s)` with the property and `0 Warning(s)`
    without it, so there is no backlog the switch would turn into a wall. It
    then became a separate decision with its own measurement, which is ADR-0093,
    and ADR-0092's `_warnaserror` globalconfig item is discussed there too.
    Measured default: false.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks>net10.0</NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks>
    <NamiApplicationTargetFramework>net10.0</NamiApplicationTargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The warning gate (ADR-0093), the analyzer breadth it reads (ADR-0094), and
    the security axis ADR-0092 section 1 already chose but could not spell.

    Measured 2026-08-03 on SDK 10.0.301, and the measurement is the reason all
    four could land at once: the solution builds `0 Warning(s)` with them and
    `0 Warning(s)` without them. There is no backlog to clear, which will not
    be true again once `src/` fills.

    AnalysisLevelSecurity is `latest-all` and NOT `all`. This is the trap in
    this group and it was found by measurement rather than expected. Measured
    2026-08-03 on SDK 10.0.301, against a project calling `MD5.HashData`: the
    bare word produced exit 0 with CA5351 never firing, even alongside
    TreatWarningsAsErrors, while `latest-all` reported it. ADR-0092 quotes the
    SDK's own comment describing the compound `<level>-<mode>` form.

    The bare word does NOT fail to parse, and knowing that is what explains why
    the failure is silent rather than an error. It parses as the LEVEL, and the
    mode it does not supply falls back to a default, so the SDK goes looking for
    a globalconfig that was never shipped. Traced through
    `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.NetAnalyzers.targets` lines 498 to 553 and then
    confirmed by evaluating the computed properties on 2026-08-03, SDK 10.0.301:

      AnalysisLevelSecurity=all           AnalysisLevelSecurity=latest-all
        PrefixSecurity   ''                 PrefixSecurity   'latest'
        SuffixSecurity   ''                 SuffixSecurity   'all'
        Effective        'all'              Effective        '10.0'
        mode             'Default'          mode             'all'
        file name        analysislevelsecurity_all_default.globalconfig
                                            analysislevelsecurity_10_all.globalconfig

    `AnalysisLevelPrefixSecurity` is only assigned when the value contains a `-`
    (targets:512), and the suffix only when the prefix is non-empty
    (targets:513), so a bare word leaves both empty; the effective level then
    takes the raw value (targets:524-525) and the mode falls through to the
    literal `Default` (targets:531-535). Every shipped file under
    `analyzers/build/config` is named for a NUMERIC level, 5 through 11, so
    `ls analysislevelsecurity_all*` in that directory matches nothing. The
    include is guarded by `Exists()` (targets:551), so MSBuild rejects nothing,
    logs nothing, and applies no configuration. That is the whole reason this
    misspelling is silent instead of loud.

    That measurement was taken against a project with NO AnalysisMode set, and
    the scope matters if it is ever re-run from here. CA5351 is also a
    Recommended-tier rule (`analysislevel_10_recommended.globalconfig` carries
    `dotnet_diagnostic.CA5351.severity = warning`), so under the group below it
    fires from either axis and cannot tell the two apart. Counted 2026-08-03 in
    the globalconfigs shipped with SDK 10.0.301, 70 of the 94 rules in
    `analysislevelsecurity_10_all.globalconfig` are NOT in the Recommended set,
    the CA3xxx taint family among them, and only one of those 70 can show
    whether this line is doing anything. Every count and line number in this
    paragraph and the next is read in that SDK and moves with it.

    `scripts/test-warnings-as-errors.sh` Part 3 is that proof, and it uses
    CA5392 (line 252 of the security globalconfig, absent from the Recommended
    and default ones) against an `internal` DllImport. Its comment carries the
    measured table. Change this line to the bare `all` and that part goes red;
    it is the only thing in the tree that notices.

    CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors is deliberately NOT set. It is the
    property that selects the `_warnaserror` variant of the shipped
    globalconfig (`Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.NetAnalyzers.props:22` feeds it into
    `EffectiveCodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors`, and `.targets:18-19` is where
    that chooses the suffix), and it has no SDK default: measured 2026-08-03 on
    SDK 10.0.301 against a project outside this repository, it evaluates to the
    empty string rather than to `false`. Both routes were measured on the same
    day and SDK reaching exit 1 on the same CA5351 violation, so setting it as
    well would be two
    mechanisms for one outcome, and TreatWarningsAsErrors covers every axis
    rather than one.

    WarningsNotAsErrors carves out the four NuGet audit codes, and the
    `$(...)` prefix preserves anything another file sets, so this appends
    rather than replaces, the same shape as the WarningsAsErrors line above.
    NuGet audit is ON by default and at its strictest values already
    (`NuGet.targets:73-82`: NuGetAudit true, NuGetAuditLevel low,
    NuGetAuditMode all for net10.0), and `NuGet.targets:946-948` puts these
    three warning properties on the restore graph entry that `NuGet.targets:199`
    hands to the RestoreTask, so without the carve-out a newly published
    advisory reddens an unchanged tree AT RESTORE, before anything can be built
    to investigate it. Those three NuGet.targets line numbers are read in SDK
    10.0.301 and move with the SDK.

    Measured 2026-08-03 on SDK 10.0.301 against System.Text.Encodings.Web
    4.5.0, restoring for net10.0. This is the one figure here that depends on a
    live advisory as well as on the SDK, so it is the first to go stale:

      no TreatWarningsAsErrors      warning NU1904, exit 0
      TreatWarningsAsErrors, no
        carve-out                   error NU1904: Warning As Error, exit 1
      TreatWarningsAsErrors, with
        the carve-out below         NU1904 back to warning

    The third row is why the carve-out is four codes and not a blanket. NU1510,
    the package-pruning advisory, is raised by the same restore and stays an
    error under the carve-out, so the restore still exits 1. That is intended
    and ADR-0093 section C says so: NU1510's remedy is available immediately,
    and it is the absence of an available remedy that earns an exemption.

    Blocking on dependency vulnerabilities is ADR-0092 stage 2's job, not this
    file's.

    Do not add a fifth code to this list to make a build pass. ADR-0093
    parameter D routes an exemption to a per-project NoWarn with a comment
    naming what and why.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsNotAsErrors>
    <AnalysisLevelSecurity>latest-all</AnalysisLevelSecurity>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
